    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "bc07b3ba-b34c-52e8-9bb6-7395260b6c25",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $(5+x^2)\\left(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}-4y\\right)+x{y}^{′}={e}^{x},\\,y(4)=3$\n- **II.** $(5+x^2)\\left(\\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2}-2y\\right)+x{y}^{′}={e}^{x},\\,y(2)=-2,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=-1$\n- **III.** $(4+x^2)\\left(\\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2}-4y\\right)+x{y}^{′}={e}^{x}$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $(5+x^2)\\left(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}-4y\\right)+x{y}^{′}={e}^{x}$ together with the initial condition $y(4)=3.$\n\n- System II is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $(5+x^2)\\left(\\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2}-2y\\right)+x{y}^{′}={e}^{x}$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(2)=-2,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=-1.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. We have here a second-order ordinary differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "9ce16e8a-fb12-5edb-90aa-a3e3af5e02c2",
